<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Osred I of Northumbria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 697–716</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Osred I (c. 697–716) became king of Northumbria as a boy of about eight on the death of his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aldfrith-of-northumbria">Aldfrith</a> in 705, after a disputed succession. His reign was later remembered for violence and the abuse of nuns, according to a letter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a>. He was killed in 716, and the throne passed to Coenred of a rival branch of the royal house.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Osred was the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aldfrith-of-northumbria">Aldfrith of Northumbria</a>. Aldfrith's only known wife was Cuthburh, but it is not certain that she was Osred's mother. Because of his youth Osred did not succeed his father directly: Eadwulf I of Northumbria seized the throne and held it for only a few months before he was overthrown. Osred's own reign is counted from February or March 705 at the latest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>While Osred was a child, the government was controlled by Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a>, presumably with the help of ealdormen such as Berhtfrith son of Berhtred, and Wilfrid adopted the boy as his son. Wilfrid's death in 709 appears to have caused no instability, which, with the quick fall of Eadwulf, suggests a degree of stability in early 8th-century Northumbria that did not long outlast Osred. In 711 Berhtfrith inflicted a crushing defeat on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> around the upper Forth; otherwise the reign was politically uneventful.</p><p>Church writers judged the young king harshly. A variety of ecclesiastical sources portray him as dissolute and a seducer of nuns, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> called him a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah">Josiah</a>. Æthelwulf's early ninth-century poem De Abbatibus describes him as energetic in deeds and words, mighty in arms and bold in his own strength, but also as rash, foolish and unable to control his desires, and as a tyrant who forced many political enemies into monastic communities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Osred reached his majority in 715 or 716 and was killed very soon afterwards. The manner of his death is unclear. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> says only that he was killed south of the border; the historians David Rollason and N.J. Higham take this to mean the southern border of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>, and presume that the Picts killed him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Osred I of Northumbria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
